second artist, birth date for a different person, or second sport is evidence to retain it. Check each proposed deletion once more against all originals before producing the final short list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"What is the genre of \\\"The Hot Rock\\\"?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 153, \"text\": \"The Hot Rock (album) The Hot Rock is the fourth studio album by the American rock band Sleater-Kinney, released on February 23, 1999, by Kill Rock Stars.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00\", \"start\": 154, \"end\": 214, \"text\": \"It was produced by Roger Moutenot and recorded at the Avast!\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00\", \"start\": 215, \"end\": 268, \"text\": \"recording studio in Seattle, Washington in July 1998.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00\", \"start\": 269, \"end\": 435, \"text\": \"\\\"The Hot Rock\\\" marks a considerable change in the band's sound, veering into a more relaxed and gloomy direction than the raucous punk rock style of its predecessors.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00\", \"start\": 436, \"end\": 532, \"text\": \"The lyrical themes of the album explore issues of failed relationships and personal uncertainty.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00\", \"start\": 533, \"end\": 592, \"text\": \"Upon release, \\\"The Hot Rock\\\" reached number 181 on the U.S.\"}, \"D1S7\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00\", \"start\": 593, \"end\": 604, \"text\": \"\\\"Billboard\\\"\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 156, \"text\": \"The Hot Rock (film) The Hot Rock is a 1972 American comedy-drama caper film directed by Peter Yates from a screenplay by William Goldman, based on Donald E.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d01\", \"start\": 157, \"end\": 252, \"text\": \"Westlake's novel of the same name, which introduced his long-running John Dortmunder character.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d01\", \"start\": 253, \"end\": 349, \"text\": \"The film stars Robert Redford, George Segal, Ron Leibman, Paul Sand, Moses Gunn and Zero Mostel.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d01\", \"start\": 350, \"end\": 504, \"text\": \"After John Dortmunder (Redford) is released from his latest stint in prison, he is approached by his brother-in-law, Andy Kelp (Segal), about another job.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d01\", \"start\": 505, \"end\": 508, \"text\": \"Dr.\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d01\", \"start\": 509, \"end\": 609, \"text\": \"Amusa (Gunn) seeks a valuable gem in the Brooklyn Museum that is of great significance to his people\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"The Hot Rock\", \"scope\": \"album by Sleater-Kinney\", \"claim\": \"The Hot Rock veers into a more relaxed and gloomy direction than the raucous punk rock style of its predecessors.\", \"answer\": \"relaxed and gloomy direction\", \"quote\": \"\\\"The Hot Rock\\\" marks a considerable change in the band's sound, veering into a more relaxed and gloomy direction than the raucous punk rock style of its predecessors.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0293-fe4c552296d4:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"The Hot Rock (film)\", \"scope\": \"genre\", \"claim\": \"The Hot Rock is a 1972 American comedy-drama caper film.\", \"answer\": \"comedy-drama caper film\", \"quote\": \"The Hot Rock (film) The Hot Rock is a 1972 American comedy-drama caper film directed by Peter Yates from a screenplay by William Goldman, based on Donald E.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}], \"R5_answers\": [\"comedy-drama caper film\", \"relaxed and gloomy direction\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"comedy-drama caper film\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"relaxed and gloomy direction\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C2\", \"answer\": \"punk rock\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C3\", \"answer\": \"rock\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C4\", \"answer\": \"comedy-drama\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C5\", \"answer\": \"caper film\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C6\", \"answer\": \"gloomy\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C7\", \"answer\": \"relaxed\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C8\", \"answer\": \"raucous punk rock\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C9\", \"answer\": \"American rock\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C10\", \"answer\": \"American comedy-drama caper film\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C11\", \"answer\": \"1972 American comedy-drama caper film\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C12\", \"answer\": \"1999 album\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}, {\"id\": \"C13\", \"answer\": \"studio album\", \"origin\": \"proposal\"}]}"
